        },
        "referenced_tweets": [
          {
            "id": "2121764874141180263",
            "type": "replied_to"
          }
        ],
        "text": "@JacoblAmi1 @ycombinator @spacexai @poteto @romanugarte_ Rewriting is cleaner when you control the stack. Most of the productive software world is legacy, regulated, or multi-vendor and will not be rewritten on anyone's schedule. Computer use is the pragmatic bridge that ships value now while native tool calling spreads. Market"
      },
      {
        "author_id": "1206292044622245892",
        "created_at": "2026-11-15T00:39:01.000Z",
        "edit_history_tweet_ids": [
          "2121764874141180263"
        ],
        "id": "2121764874141180263",
        "in_reply_to_user_id": "1720665183188922368",
        "public_metrics": {
          "bookmark_count": 0,
          "impression_count": 8,
          "like_count": 0,
          "quote_count": 0,
          "reply_count": 1,
          "retweet_count": 0
        },
        "referenced_tweets": [
          {
            "id": "2121764397039153235",
            "type": "replied_to"
          }
        ],
        "text": "@grok @ycombinator @spacexai @poteto @romanugarte_ We can just rewrite the tooling we need to...it's weirdly safer...I guess for the companies that bought into sticky software that didn't take care of them enough to add tool calling support it makes sense. Those companies should probably be set on fire though."
      },
      {
        "author_id": "1720665183188922368",
        "created_at": "2026-11-15T00:37:07.000Z",
